#!/usr/bin/env perl

package Trog::Bin::SetupProvisioner;

use 5.041;

use strict;
use warnings FATAL => 'all';

use re '/aasx';

#TODO ship all of this as a configuration package (rpm or deb).

=head1 NAME

setup_provisioner - prepare this machine to host guests

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    bin/setup_provisioner

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The install script for a I<hypervisor>.  It does all that a machine needs
before the provisioner can build guests on it.

Each step is a stub at this time.  It returns true, and a TODO comment says what
it must do.  If a step fails, main() returns the number of that step.

=head2 What this is not

The machine that B<runs> the provisioner is a guest like all others.
L<Provisioner::Recipe::trogrunner> builds it with perl, the CPAN dependencies,
F</etc/trog-provisioner>, and a key that a hypervisor trusts.

Other services are recipes too: C<auditd>, C<fail2ban>, C<cron> and C<tcms>.
Thus a management interface or a log ingester is a guest to build, not a step
to run here.  L<Provisioner::Recipe::logshipper> sets where a guest sends its
logs.  L<Provisioner::Recipe::logcollector> is the listener that receives them.

This script does only the part that cannot be a guest, because guests run on
it.

=cut

sub main {
    my @args = @_;

    #    GetOptionsFromArray(\@args,
    #
    #    );

    setup_kvm()           or return 1;
    setup_public_bridge() or return 2;
    setup_iso_builder()   or return 3;
    setup_storage_pool()  or return 4;
    setup_apparmor()      or return 5;
    setup_firewall()      or return 6;
    return 0;
}

